<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="a7a6b792">&lt;p&gt;
    The project management roles guide defines a consistent set of project management roles and governance groups that are
    used to deliver a project within EY. Clearly defining roles and project governance helps a project be well-executed and
    meet its objectives on time and within budget.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Clearly defining a project organization helps to deliver a project within scope and quality, on time and within budget.
    A project organization defines the project management roles and governance groups that are necessary for a project to
    be well executed and meet its objectives.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    This guide is used to understand how a project organization is created. The guide provides definitions for standard
    project roles, and outlines which roles are required versus optional for various sized projects. The roles and
    governance groups are based on the key stakeholders suggested by the project management Institute (PMI) and have been
    customized to fit EY&apos;s culture.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Important note: The roles and governance groups are assigned to participants for the duration of a project. They are
    not necessarily the same as job titles or EY organizational roles. In addition to project management roles, EY defines
    account and engagement team roles. Delivering targeted services in a complex environment means that EY practitioners
    may fulfill several roles simultaneously, depending on the situation.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Project organization&lt;br /&gt;
    A project will have both a requesting organization and a performing organization where:&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    The requesting organization is responsible for obtaining project funding, defining the project objectives and accepting
    the final result of the project.&lt;br /&gt;
    The performing organization creates the result of the project. It is responsible for managing the work of the project,
    which includes performing project management as well as producing project deliverables that meet the project
    objectives.&lt;br /&gt;
    The following diagram shows the standard project roles and typical governance groups for a project:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/PM07.jpg&quot; width=&quot;452&quot; height=&quot;478&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Standard roles for a project&lt;br /&gt;
    A project will have the following six standard project roles:&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    1. Executive Project Sponsor — Individual assigned by the requesting organization who acquires project funding.&lt;br /&gt;
    2. Managing Project Sponsor — Individual assigned by the requesting organization who provides support and tactical
    guidance to the Project Manager.&lt;br /&gt;
    3. Project Manager (PM) — Individual assigned by the performing organization who manages the project to achieve project
    objectives.&lt;br /&gt;
    4. Project management team — Individuals assigned primarily by the performing organization who participate in project
    management activities.&lt;br /&gt;
    5. Project team — Individuals assigned primarily by the performing organization who perform activities and tasks
    defined for the project in support of project objectives.&lt;br /&gt;
    6. Stakeholder group — Individuals or groups assigned by the requesting organization and performing organization who
    represent the vested interest of their group on a project.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Governance groups for a project&lt;br /&gt;
    Here are the typical governance structures to be applied as appropriate to projects:&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    1. Project steering group — Provides guidance and decisions regarding project direction and changes affecting project
    outcomes which includes resolution of escalated issues, risks and conflicts.&lt;br /&gt;
    2. Project change control board — Has decision-making authority on resolution of major-impact Project Change Requests
    (PCRs).&lt;br /&gt;
    3. Project management office — Provides support to the Project Manager on project management activities.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Usage of project roles and governance groups by project classification&lt;br /&gt;
    The structure applied to a project varies by complexity and organization. Usage of project roles and governance groups
    should be determined based upon project need, risk to the organization and project classification – refer to chart for
    guidance.
&lt;/p&gt;
